## A realistic invoice layout

A commercial invoice template typically follows this structure:

```text
Page

┌────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Company Logo      TAX INVOICE      │
│ Company Details                    │
│                                    │
│ Invoice Number                     │
│ Issue Date                         │
│ Due Date                           │
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Bill To                            │
│ Customer Name                      │
│ Address                            │
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Qty Description Unit Tax Amount    │
│                                    │
│ ...                                │
│ ...                                │
│ ...                                │
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│                    Subtotal         │
│                    GST              │
│                    Total            │
├────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Payment Terms                      │
│ Bank Details                       │
│ Notes                              │
└────────────────────────────────────┘
```

For a project like KelpieBooks, I'd aim to keep **Typst in charge of layout** and **MiniJinja in charge of data insertion**. That means very little logic in the template beyond loops and simple conditionals, with formatting (dates, currency, quantities, tax labels) handled by MiniJinja filters supplied from Rust. This separation tends to produce templates that are easy for designers to modify without touching application code.
